<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Dev-only manifest overlay: REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES, declared here and nowhere else, so a release
  build cannot hold it.

  This permission is not held to install anything silently. It is held because the platform's own
  installer refuses to run a confirmation flow for an app that may not install packages. Measured both
  ways on the handset with identical code, identical apk and identical signing:

    declared and allowed  -> session commits, status STATUS_PENDING_USER_ACTION, and
                             com.android.packageinstaller/.PackageInstallerActivity comes up with
                             "Do you want to update this app?"; tapping Update replaces the app.
    not declared          -> session commits, status STATUS_PENDING_USER_ACTION, an installer component
                             resolves, startActivity() does not throw, and no dialog is ever shown. The
                             session then sits unanswered until the platform abandons it.

  The failure mode of the undeclared case is silent, which is why the coordinator refuses to stage
  without the right rather than reporting a confirmation that will never appear.

  com.termux.anchor.permission.CONTROL is here for the same reason the install right is: it is the
  signature-level permission the supervisor anchor APK guards its heartbeat receiver with, so a build
  that does not declare it cannot report to the anchor at all. Declaring it costs a release build nothing
  except the truth - the release manifest would then be claiming a relationship with a package that is not
  in the release train - and DevKitHeartbeat does not send anything unless BuildConfig.DEVKIT_HOST, which
  is false outside the dev variant anyway. Keeping it in this overlay is what makes "a release build never
  mentions the anchor" true of the manifest as well as of the code path.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />

    <!-- Held so that DevKitHeartbeat's com.termux.anchor.HEARTBEAT broadcast is delivered. Granted by the
         platform on install because both APKs are signed with the same release key, which is the whole
         authentication: see termux-anchor/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="com.termux.anchor.permission.CONTROL" />

    <!--
      Package visibility for the heartbeat in DevKitHeartbeat. Today this changes nothing measurable:
      filtering applies to apps targeting SDK 30+, and this app is held at 28, so getPackageInfo already
      answered for the anchor on the handset. It is declared anyway because the failure without it is the
      worst shape of failure this project has seen - the day targetSdk rises, the anchor still looks
      absent, the heartbeat quietly never starts, and supervision stops existing with nothing in any log.
    -->
    <queries>
        <package android:name="com.termux.anchor" />
    </queries>

</manifest>
